Notebook[{Cell["Reading the book's diagrams automatically", "Title"], 
  Cell["A program that turns the figures of Turaev & Virelizier into layered \
forms, and the simplifier that checks what it read", "Subtitle"], 
  Cell["By Dara O Shayda and Noor Hoshino (\:661f\:91ce\:30cc\:30fc\:30eb) \
\[CenterDot] \[Copyright] Computational Classnotes \[CenterDot] CC BY-NC-SA \
4.0", "Text"], Cell["The pictures of the book's pages in this notebook are \
from Turaev & Virelizier, Monoidal Categories and Topological Field Theory \
(Progress in Mathematics 322, Springer 2017), \[Copyright] Springer \
International Publishing AG 2017, shown as short excerpts for study and commentary beside the book.", "Text"], Cell["1. The idea", "Section"], 
  Cell["The notebook reading-diagrams-from-the-book.nb shows a figure being \
read by eye and then checked. Here the reading is done by a program. The \
book's PDF is not a scan: each figure is stored as drawing instructions. A \
strand is a line segment or a chain of B\[EAcute]zier curves, a box is a \
rectangle, an arrow head is a small filled shape, and every label (X, f, \
\[Omega], ev_Y, \[Or]X) is real text with a position on the page. So the \
program does not have to recognise shapes in pixels or letters in an image: \
it reads the instructions and rebuilds the diagram from them.", "Text"], 
  Cell["The reader is figreader.py (tools/figure-reader in the repository). \
It needs Python with PyMuPDF (pip install pymupdf). On this Mac python3 is \
only Apple's placeholder, so the readings were made once with the reader and \
saved in book-figures/figure-readings.json; this notebook loads that file. To \
read again:", "Text"], Cell["python3 figreader.py \
monoidal-categories-and-topological-field-theory_compress.pdf 44 45 46 \
\[Ellipsis] 100 > book-figures/figure-readings.json", "Program"], 
  Cell["2. How the reader works", "Section"], Cell["1. Primitives. For one \
page: every stroked path becomes a polyline (curves sampled at 17 points, \
lines every half point); rectangles with a border are boxes; small filled \
shapes are arrow heads; text spans keep their positions and font sizes.", 
   "Item"], Cell["2. Pictures. Paths and boxes that touch are one connected \
picture. Pictures on one row are split into the sides of equations by the \
signs between them: = joins two sides, and, or and commas end an equation, \
and so does a wide gap with no sign. Pictures between two signs are one side \
(f and g in f \[CircleTimes] g are two pictures, one side).", "Item"], 
  Cell["3. Strands. A path is cut where it runs behind a box, and then at \
every turning point in height. The pieces are strand segments; a turning \
point at the top is a cap (ev or \:1ebdv), at the bottom a cup (coev or \
c\:0303oev). A piece whose lower end lies on the top edge of a box is an \
output of that box; one whose upper end lies on the bottom edge is an input. \
A piece with a free lower end is a source.", "Item"], 
  Cell["4. Labels. Text inside a box is its name; a smaller span below the \
line is a subscript, \:2217 and \[Prime] are kept, \[Or] is a prefix. Other \
text is grouped into words and each word goes to the nearest strand: that is \
the colour of the whole strand (one path through caps and cups).", "Item"], 
  Cell["5. Arrows (\[Section]2.1.3). For each arrow head the tip and the back \
are found from its vertices, and compared with the direction of the path at \
that point. Then every piece of the path is oriented, up or down. A piece of \
colour X pointing down contributes X, pointing up X*. A cap whose left leg is \
X* and right leg X is ev_X; with X and X* it is \:1ebdv_X; cups likewise give \
coev_X and c\:0303oev_X.", "Item"], Cell["6. Layers. The boxes, caps and cups \
are swept from the bottom of the picture to the top, keeping the list of \
strands that cross the current height, left to right. Each one takes its \
inputs, which must be side by side in that list (their position is a), and \
puts its outputs in their place. The result is the layered form {src, {{a, \
name, ins, outs}, \[Ellipsis]}}.", "Item"], Cell["Every step is geometry on \
exact coordinates; nothing is guessed from the text of the book. The checks \
below then decide whether a reading can be trusted.", "Text"], 
  Cell["3. Setup", "Section"], Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \
\"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \
\"figure-reader.wl\"}]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["eqs = importReadings[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \
\"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"book-figures\", \
\"figure-readings.json\"}]]; Length[eqs]", "Input"], 
  Cell[BoxData["182"], "Output"], Cell["One reading, as the reader wrote it: \
the page, and for each side the bounding box on the page, the source colours, \
and the layers.", "Text"], Cell["eqs[[6]]", "Input"], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
      RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"page\"", "\[Rule]", "44"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"\"sides\"", "\[Rule]", RowBox[
           {"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", RowBox[
                 {RowBox[{"\"bbox\"", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[
                       {"142.9`", ",", "420.1`", ",", "176.`", ",", 
                        "471.1`"}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"\"src\"", 
                    "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"\"X\"", ",", "\"U\""}], 
                      "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"\"ls\"", "\[Rule]", RowBox[
                     {"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "\"f\"", 
                          ",", RowBox[{"{", "\"X\"", "}"}], ",", RowBox[
                          {"{", "\"Y\"", "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[
                         {"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "\"g\"", ",", RowBox[{"{", 
                          "\"U\"", "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", "\"V\"", "}"}]}], 
                          "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"\"error\"", 
                    "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}], ",", 
              RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"bbox\"", 
                    "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"202.4`", ",", "420.1`", 
                        ",", "235.6`", ",", "471.1`"}], "}"}]}], ",", 
                  RowBox[{"\"src\"", "\[Rule]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"\"X\"", 
                        ",", "\"U\""}], "}"}]}], ",", RowBox[{"\"ls\"", 
