   "Output"], Cell["5.1 Why the others are not confirmed", "Subsection"], 
  Cell["Proofs written as chains: the uniqueness of the inverse (p. 33) needs \
two pairings, and the zigzag rule then cancels either one, so \
\[CapitalOmega]\[Prime] and \[CapitalOmega] keep different normal forms. That \
is the content of the statement, not a reading error.", "Item"], 
  Cell["Rules that are not automatic: the trace symmetry tr(pq) = tr(qp) \
chain on p. 47, the \[Psi] and \[CapitalPsi]_S boxes of \
\[Section]2.3\[Dash]2.4, turning \[Omega]_S in \[Section]4.3.2 (a Chapter 4 \
term rule, which needs the term form).", "Item"], 
  Cell["Labels split into pieces: tensor colours written across several spans \
(Y \[CircleTimes] X), labels on two lines, and subscripts with stars (X_1*, \
X_S*). The reader keeps the pieces apart, so it names the strand \
\[CircleTimes]X.", "Item"], Cell["What the reader does not know yet: \
crossings (\[Tau], \[Section]3.1 on) are drawn as two paths with a gap and \
are not joined; the red partition shapes of Chapter 4 are filled regions, not \
boxes; curls through crossings (twists).", "Item"], 
  Cell["6. When a reading is wrong, the checks notice", "Section"], 
  Cell["The first picture of ?*\:2082(X, Y) on p. 35 carries the tensor \
labels Y \[CircleTimes] X and X \[CircleTimes] Y. The reader names every \
strand, so the reading counts as complete, but it splits the labels and \
invents a colour \[CircleTimes]X:", "Text"], 
